ls", "contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}]}], "questions": [{"responses": [{"selected": 0, "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct": 1}], "_data": [{"type": "question", "contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09re7pdf01.pdf"}]}], "id": 0}], "template_override": 1}], "type": "scholar", "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "id": 0}, {"type": "audio", "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe8n3le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Professor</span> : I'm glad to hear your presentation about web pages. Are you ready? <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Student 1</span> : Yes, first, if you begin a new page, you have to include a declaration. This tells the browser that the following file is an HTML file. <br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Professor</span> : I'm sorry to interrupt you but, can you explain how they are structured?<br />\n<span class=\"strong\">Student 2</span> : Right, HTML has two main parts: the head and the body. If you build a new webpage, you need to consider these tags: < html >, < head >, < title > and < body > which are container tags. When you use container tags you need to have a beginning and an end.", "image": "enpe8n3le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "time": "0", "id": 0}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Student 1</span>: As you know, when you do an HTML document, you always have to begin and end the document with a tag. This is because they inform the browser how to display the information. Then, the < head > includes general information about the file, for example, the author, copyright, and keywords.\n<br /><span class=\"strong\">Student 2</span>: In the head part, you also find the title part. Titles have to be descriptive. So, make sure they don't have more than 20 words. Finally, the body: text images, links, and some other relevant information you want to include.", "image": "enpe8n3le03ob09re2img02.jpg", "time": "52000", "id": 1}, {"text": "<span class=\"strong\">Student 1</span>: Nesting is a very important part of the web structure. If you have a new set of tags, you need to nest them inside other tags. In this case, for example, the tag title, and head are nested and the body is nested inside HTLM.\n<br /><span class=\"strong\">Professor</span>: Now we are prepared to design a webpage.", "image": "enpe8n3le03ob09re2img03.jpg", "time": "91000", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe8n3le03ob09re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "_data": [], "id": 1, "template_override": 1}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}]}], "type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What is The Structure of HTML?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about the structure of HTML"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Interpret the requirements report to determine the technological needs in managing information, based on rules and protocols established by the company."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Participates in simple conversations or simulated situations about the communicative topics for this level through the appropriate use of simple pronunciation aspects, and grammar and vocabulary structures.  Recognizes simple ideas on requirement reports, tools for Object Oriented Programming, and other basic programming tools."}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adjectives + Infinitives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use infinitive verbs after adjectives when you want to express feelings or how you react to things."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Remember that <span class=\"strong\">infinitives</span> almost always begin with the word to and after them you write the simple form of the verb which is not conjugated. It means that for the infinitive verbs you don't have to add <span class=\"strong\">s,</span> or <span class=\"strong\">es, ed</span> or <span class=\"strong\">ing</span> at the end. For example, to eat, to play and to work.<br /><br />  Adjectives are words that you use to describe things, people, animals, places. For example: happy, big, small, and interesting.<br /><br />  You can use an adjective and after an infinitive verb in a sentence, when you want to specify a feeling or a reaction to something.<br /><br />  Examples:<br /> <span class=\"italic\">I am <span class=\"strong\">happy to see</span> you.<br /> She was <span class=\"strong\">afraid to meet</span> him.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm glad to hear your presentation about web pages."}, {"type": "example", "content": "So, are you ready to present the rules and main parts?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm sorry to interrupt you but can you explain how they are structured."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Zero Conditional to Talk About a Specific Topic, and Verbs Plus Infinitives in the Main Clause"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the zero conditional to talk about a situation that is real or to mention rules. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use this structure to explain two situations that complement each other and that are always real. The first part explains a condition. The second explains the result, consequence or direct relation to the first condition. Both parts need to be in present simple.<br /><br />  After the main clause, you can use infinitives: <br />   If (main clause) + Present Simple (infinitive)<br />  Do not forget to keep the structure of infinitives.<br /><br />  Example:<br /> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span> you create a web page, you <span class=\"strong\">have to use</span> tags.<br /> <span class=\"strong\">If</span> you write codes, you <span class=\"strong\">need to be</span> careful.</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "So, if you build a new webpage, you need to consider these tags: <html>, <head>, <title> and <body>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you begin a new page, you have to include a declaration."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "willing "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "feeling enthusiastic   "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:   We're willing to learn everything about the structures of HTML."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "declaration "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an explicit statement of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:   Well, first thing, if you begin a new page, you have to include a declaration."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "copyright   "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the legal right that an author has for the creation of something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Then, the \u201c< head >\u201d includes general information about the file, for example, the author, copyright and keywords."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "nesting"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to fit one thing inside another."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Nesting is a very important part of the web structure. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "structure   "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to organize something in a structure"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I'm sorry to interrupt you but can you explain how they are structured."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "comprehend "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to include"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:   Finally, the body comprehends the text images, links, and some other relevant information you want to include."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n3le03ob09aud06"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Find out information about the structure of HTML. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a mind map giving examples about how the tags are organized in the structure. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share you work with your classmates and check pronunciation with your instructor."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I explain the structure of HTML.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe with examples, how the tags are organized in the structure. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use different grammar structures to give examples about how tags are organized."}]}]}